#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097846 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. How can we plot the graph of $y=-f(x)$?\n\n![](images/5b/5bab57ba6ac7cd9b0ea80287457ddc7ee50cadf2580a84a802fa039bbca27e1a.png)\n\nAs usual, It's helpful to first find a few points on the graph of $y=-f(x)$. You can use the points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ to identify corresponding points on the graph of $y=-f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points ony = f(x) & Points ony =-f(x) \\\\ (-1, 2) & (-1,-2) \\\\ (0,-1) & (0, 1) \\\\ (2, 0) & (2, 0) \\\\ (3, 4) & (3,-4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a point on the graph of $y=-f(x)$ with the *same* $x$-value and the *opposite* $y$-value.\n\nLet's plot our new points, and draw $y=-f(x)$.\n\n![](images/5a/5a1e6c5a95dff6b8e1f7eba5de197ba61f0ae64a94db2eeb57fc6cd23661f877.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=-f(x)$ is a **reflection across the $x$-axis** of the graph of $y=f(x)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "47f1f319-6405-55bb-b469-23e90a480d3c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3281 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
